  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "2.10.0: What an integration like Microsoft.Extensions.VectorData needs, done by the client. GetCollectionClient(name) works on whichever collection has the name, also after it is created again; DeleteCollectionIfExistsAsync takes a missing collection as deleted. In UpdateAsync and UpsertAsync a null value or an empty list deletes the key and the sparse vectors of its text, sent only to the records that have it, and ChromaRecords.NullDocumentsDelete deletes the documents; ChromaCollectionClient.WithDocumentCopyKey copies each document into a metadata key, for a where filter on the whole text, and reads a deleted document as null and an empty one as empty. ChromaRecords takes null metadata and documents, record by record, and ChromaMetadataConvert.ToMetadata builds the metadata of a record from values that can be null. ChromaWhereOperator.Not, All and None, and ChromaWhereDocumentOperator.Not, do what Chroma has no operator for; In and NotIn without values are None and All. ChromaWhereOperator filters the ids with ChromaSearchKeys.Id and the documents with Document, as the Search API does, and the client sends them to get, query and delete as their ids and where_document. ChromaCollectionSchema.WithBm25Index declares a BM25 index on the text of a key, with ifSupported only on Chroma Cloud, the only server with sparse vector indexes, and ChromaCollectionClient.FindBm25IndexAsync finds it; the results of ChromaRank.HybridRrf come with the fused score, highest first. Also ChromaQuery.Offset and ExpectedSpace, ChromaRank.HybridRrf, ChromaCollection.FindBm25Index, ChromaCollectionClient.WithMetadataValues, ChromaMetadataConvert and ChromaCloudQuotas. A query whose ids include some without a record leaves them out, as Chroma Cloud does, instead of failing on Chroma 1.x; with deleteRecordsFirst, deleting a collection or a database deletes its records first, so that Chroma 1.5 gives their lists to no other record. GetOrCreateCollectionAsync throws when the collection exists with another space, and on Chroma Cloud the creation of a collection throws for a key of the schema beyond 36 bytes, which Chroma Cloud would reject on every write. All the releases: https://github.com/ChromaDotNet/ChromaDB.Client/releases. Tested versions: https://github.com/ChromaDotNet/ChromaDB.Client/blob/main/docs/COMPATIBILITY.md. Migration from 1.x: https://github.com/ChromaDotNet/ChromaDB.Client#migrating-from-chromadbclient-1x",
